      "type": "p",
      "text": "Fair Lawn's postwar chimneys were usually flashed in aluminum, and most of that has been replaced at least once during a roof job. The problem is how: a single piece of metal laid flat against the brick and sealed with roofing tar. Tar hardens in the sun, pulls away from the brick, and leaves a gap that every rain runs behind. On the capes the chimney is often on the rear slope with nothing to divert water around it, so leaves and water pile against the back wall and work under the shingles."
