      "type": "p",
      "lead": "The repair:",
      "text": "we photograph and measure every shaft, the cap, and the pattern before anything comes down. The cluster is scaffolded with protective cribbing — on the slate roofs these houses have, extensively — and the shafts are taken down to sound brick, which is often the full height above the base. Each shaft is rebuilt to its original dimensions in reclaimed or color-matched soft brick, laid in a lime-rich mortar, with the pattern reproduced course by course and the flue tile continued inside it. The shafts are tied to each other with stainless ties at intervals, which the original mason didn't have, and the corbeled cap is rebuilt over a poured, reinforced crown that spans the cluster, with a bond break around each flue. Each flue gets a cap sized to it. The result is the chimney the house was built with, standing straighter than it did."
