      "type": "p",
      "text": "A false chimney fails differently from a real one. A real chimney is a solid masonry column from the basement up, warmed from inside by the flue, and water that gets in at the top has a long way to go. A false chimney is a hollow brick shell sitting on the roof framing or on a wood box in the attic, with a thin mortar wash or a slab on top, and no mass to absorb or shed anything. Water goes in at the top and lands directly on the attic framing. The brick is often a single wythe laid over a wood frame, so when the mortar goes, there's nothing holding it but the frame."
