      "text": "the top is closed properly — a poured, reinforced crown over the shell, sloped and overhanging, sealed, with no opening — and the shell is repointed like any brick. If the frame under it has rotted, the shell comes off, the frame is rebuilt or replaced with block, and the brick is re-laid on it. If the owner doesn't want to maintain a chimney that does nothing, it can come down and the roof be closed in matched slate or shingle — though on a Georgian house the symmetry is usually worth keeping, and we say so. Either way, the real chimney at the other end gets the same inspection, because it's the same age."
